{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"078b3ea079904a99","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a `Route Table`?**|What is a `Route Table`?]]","x":-100,"y":0,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"b08910b018a81176","type":"text","text":"A `Route Table` is a set of rules that controls where network traffic goes inside a `VPC`.  \nA `Route Table` is connected to a `subnet`. When resources inside that `subnet` send traffic, AWS checks the `Route Table` to decide where the traffic should go.\n```text\nDestination    Target\n10.0.0.0/16    local\n0.0.0.0/0      Internet Gateway\n```\nThe first part is `Destination`.  \nThe second part is `Target`.  \n`Destination` means where the traffic wants to go.  \n`Target` means which AWS component should receive and forward that traffic.  ","x":350,"y":0,"width":600,"height":325},
		{"id":"af6d2885788a4701","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What does `Destination` mean?**|What does `Destination` mean?]]","x":-100,"y":385,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"8864496ad3dd4dca","type":"text","text":"`Destination` means the IP range that the traffic is trying to reach.  \nExample:\n```text\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis means the traffic is going to an IP address inside the `VPC`.  \nAnother example:\n```text\n0.0.0.0/0\n```\nThis means all other traffic, usually traffic going to the internet or outside the `VPC`.  ","x":350,"y":385,"width":600,"height":320},
		{"id":"be35817d51fbe02f","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What does `Target` mean?**|What does `Target` mean?]]","x":-100,"y":765,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"88a096c84e4cd5de","type":"text","text":"`Target` means where AWS should send the traffic.  \nExample:\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\n```\nHere, `local` means the traffic stays inside the same `VPC`.  \nAnother example:\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      Internet Gateway\n```\nHere, `Internet Gateway` means the traffic goes to the internet through the `Internet Gateway`.  ","x":350,"y":765,"width":600,"height":380},
		{"id":"6509c1b0d95e47f9","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is the `local` route?**|What is the `local` route?]]","x":-100,"y":1205,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"2b28468e528a01d2","type":"text","text":"When you create a `VPC`, AWS automatically creates a `local` route.  \nExample `VPC` CIDR block:\n```text\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nAWS automatically creates this route:\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\n```\nThis route allows resources inside the same `VPC` to communicate with each other.  ","x":350,"y":1205,"width":600,"height":325},
		{"id":"2698ec4ec4ca6cc9","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**Why does AWS create the `local` route automatically?**|Why does AWS create the `local` route automatically?]]","x":-100,"y":1590,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"8f8dddc3cbc1d31d","type":"text","text":"AWS creates the `local` route so that all resources inside the same `VPC` can talk to each other.  \nExample:\n```text\nVPC CIDR: 10.0.0.0/16\nEC2 A: 10.0.1.10\nEC2 B: 10.0.2.20\n```\n`EC2 A` and `EC2 B` are in the same `VPC`.  \nEven if they are in different `subnets`, they can still communicate because of this route:\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\n```\nThis means traffic between `10.0.1.10` and `10.0.2.20` stays inside the `VPC`.  ","x":350,"y":1590,"width":600,"height":410},
		{"id":"049f9a57e5a96d3f","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What does `local` mean?**|What does `local` mean?]]","x":-100,"y":2060,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"a3223ac7a6d4e7cc","type":"text","text":"`local` means inside the same `VPC`.\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\n```\nThis route tells AWS:\n```text\nIf the destination IP is inside 10.0.0.0/16,\nkeep the traffic inside the VPC.\n```\nYou do not need to create this route yourself.  \nAWS creates it automatically.  \nYou also cannot delete or change the `local` route.  ","x":350,"y":2060,"width":600,"height":380},
		{"id":"6b05345f0453b459","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**How does a public subnet use a `Route Table`?**|How does a public subnet use a `Route Table`?]]","x":-100,"y":2500,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"e04856b5775c7f7c","type":"text","text":"A `public subnet` has a route to an `Internet Gateway`.  \nExample:\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      Internet Gateway\n```\nThe first route allows traffic inside the `VPC`.  \nThe second route allows traffic to go outside the `VPC`, usually to the internet.  \nBecause the `public subnet` has this route:\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      Internet Gateway\n```\nIt can be called a `public subnet`.  ","x":350,"y":2500,"width":600,"height":465},
		{"id":"f0ab4e459911eede","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What does `0.0.0.0/0` mean?**|What does `0.0.0.0/0` mean?]]","x":-100,"y":3025,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"fc169e4e97034e97","type":"text","text":"`0.0.0.0/0` means everywhere else.\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      Internet Gateway\n```\nThis usually means:\n```text\nTraffic that is not inside the VPC\ngoes to the Internet Gateway.\n```\nFor example, if an `EC2` instance wants to access:\n```text\ngoogle.com\ngithub.com\ndocker.com\n```\nThat traffic is outside the `VPC`, so it matches this route:\n```text\n0.0.0.0/0\n```","x":350,"y":3025,"width":600,"height":575},
		{"id":"bed7df7c332a72f4","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is an `Internet Gateway`?**|What is an `Internet Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":3660,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"66963d94db146ca0","type":"text","text":"An `Internet Gateway` is a `VPC` component that allows resources in a `public subnet` to access the internet.\n```text\nPublic Subnet\nRoute Table:\n10.0.0.0/16 → local\n0.0.0.0/0   → Internet Gateway\n```\nThe `Internet Gateway` works like an internet road for your `VPC`.  \nWithout an `Internet Gateway`, resources in the `VPC` cannot directly access the internet.  ","x":350,"y":3660,"width":600,"height":280},
		{"id":"2195f612d9fd18a1","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**Why is a subnet called public?**|Why is a subnet called public?]]","x":-100,"y":4000,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"78668263457021d9","type":"text","text":"A `subnet` is called public when its `Route Table` has a route to an `Internet Gateway`.\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      Internet Gateway\n```\nSimple idea:\n```text\nPublic subnet = has direct internet road\n```\nHowever, for an `EC2` instance to actually be reachable from the internet, it also needs other things, such as:\n```text\nPublic IP address\nSecurity Group allowing traffic\nNetwork ACL allowing traffic\nCorrect route table\n```\nSo the `Route Table` is important, but it is not the only requirement.  ","x":350,"y":4000,"width":600,"height":495},
		{"id":"da984101c98a3f4a","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**How does a private subnet use a `Route Table`?**|How does a private subnet use a `Route Table`?]]","x":-100,"y":4555,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d29afbc1a22db1a5","type":"text","text":"A `private subnet` does not have a direct route to an `Internet Gateway`.  \nBasic private subnet example:\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\n```\nThis means resources in the `private subnet` can talk to resources inside the same `VPC`.  \nBut they do not have direct internet access.  ","x":350,"y":4555,"width":600,"height":245},
		{"id":"25a32d99a399980c","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What does a private subnet route look like with a `NAT Gateway`?**|What does a private subnet route look like with a `NAT Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":4860,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"682bbcb3d7bb897e","type":"text","text":"If a private subnet needs to access the internet for updates or downloads, it can use a `NAT Gateway`.  \nExample:\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      NAT Gateway\n```\nThis allows resources in the `private subnet` to go out to the internet.  \nBut the internet cannot directly start a connection back to those private resources.  ","x":350,"y":4860,"width":600,"height":305},
		{"id":"297becaa1c23fc51","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**Why use a `NAT Gateway` for a private subnet?**|Why use a `NAT Gateway` for a private subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":5225,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"96679a9103e52c14","type":"text","text":"A `NAT Gateway` allows private resources to access the internet without making them directly public.  \nExample use cases:\n```text\nEC2 in private subnet downloads package updates\nSpring Boot backend connects to external API\nPrivate server pulls Docker image\nPrivate service sends logs to external service\n```\nThe private resource can go out.  \nBut outside users cannot directly connect to it.  ","x":350,"y":5225,"width":600,"height":305},
		{"id":"663a2c777b1bb847","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is the difference between `Internet Gateway` and `NAT Gateway`?**|What is the difference between `Internet Gateway` and `NAT Gateway`?]]","x":-100,"y":5590,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"1b19f4e8387a6f02","type":"text","text":"An `Internet Gateway` is used by a `public subnet`.  \nA `NAT Gateway` is usually used by a `private subnet`.\n```text\nPublic Subnet:\n0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway\nPrivate Subnet:\n0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway\n```\nSimple explanation:\n```text\nInternet Gateway = direct internet access\nNAT Gateway      = private outbound internet access\n```","x":350,"y":5590,"width":600,"height":390},
		{"id":"89679b25b37f636c","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is a very simple picture of `VPC`, public subnet, and private subnet?**|What is a very simple picture of `VPC`, public subnet, and private subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":6040,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"f8d7ed9c02f50bc9","type":"text","text":"Example:\n```text\nVPC: 10.0.0.0/16\nPublic Subnet\nRoute Table:\n10.0.0.0/16 → local\n0.0.0.0/0   → Internet Gateway\nPrivate Subnet\nRoute Table:\n10.0.0.0/16 → local\n```\nThis means:\n```text\nPublic subnet = has internet road\nPrivate subnet = no direct internet road\n```\nThe public subnet can send internet traffic through the `Internet Gateway`.  \nThe private subnet can only communicate inside the `VPC`, unless you add a `NAT Gateway`.  ","x":350,"y":6040,"width":600,"height":535},
		{"id":"06e15d4c9e25927e","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is the corrected private subnet example with internet access?**|What is the corrected private subnet example with internet access?]]","x":-100,"y":6635,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"60942624cae3012b","type":"text","text":"Your note says:\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget: Nat Gateway\n```\nThe idea is correct, but the better technical writing is:\n```text\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      NAT Gateway\n```\n`NAT Gateway` should be written clearly because it is an AWS service name.  \nA private subnet with outbound internet access usually has this route:\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\nDestination: 0.0.0.0/0\nTarget:      NAT Gateway\n```\nThis means traffic inside the `VPC` stays local, and traffic going outside the `VPC` goes through the `NAT Gateway`.  ","x":350,"y":6635,"width":600,"height":550},
		{"id":"c020e02620c1f298","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What route does AWS automatically create when you create a `VPC`?**|What route does AWS automatically create when you create a `VPC`?]]","x":-100,"y":7245,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"e4b4e7a0e8819584","type":"text","text":"When you create a `VPC`, AWS automatically creates this route:\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\n```\nThis route lets resources inside the `VPC` talk to each other.  \nThis works even when resources are in different `subnets`.  \nFor example:\n```text\nEC2 A in public subnet:  10.0.1.10\nEC2 B in private subnet: 10.0.2.20\n```\nThey can communicate through the `local` route, as long as `Security Group` and `Network ACL` rules allow it.  \n**Can you delete the `local` route?**  \nNo.  \nYou cannot delete or change the AWS-created `local` route.\n```text\nDestination: 10.0.0.0/16\nTarget:      local\n```\nAWS keeps this route because it is required for communication inside the `VPC`.  \n**Does every subnet need a `Route Table`?**  \nYes.  \nEvery `subnet` must use one `Route Table`.  \nA `subnet` cannot exist without being associated with a `Route Table`.  ","x":350,"y":7245,"width":600,"height":690},
		{"id":"e5236f6e2d6cdfbf","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What happens if you do not choose a `Route Table` for a subnet?**|What happens if you do not choose a `Route Table` for a subnet?]]","x":-100,"y":7995,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"2cdebc669f1b76b4","type":"text","text":"If you do not manually choose a `Route Table` for a `subnet`, AWS automatically connects that `subnet` to the `main route table`.\n```text\nSubnet without manual route table association\n→ uses main route table\n```\nSo the `main route table` is the default route table for subnets that do not have their own explicit route table association.  ","x":350,"y":7995,"width":600,"height":195},
		{"id":"3a7f769b0804707d","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**What is the `main route table`?**|What is the `main route table`?]]","x":-100,"y":8250,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"65e30970693f5ed1","type":"text","text":"The `main route table` is the default `Route Table` of a `VPC`.  \nWhen a new `VPC` is created, AWS creates a `main route table`.  \nIf a `subnet` is not manually associated with another `Route Table`, it uses the `main route table`.\n```text\nVPC\n├── Main Route Table\n├── Public Subnet  → custom public route table\n└── Private Subnet → main route table if no custom table is selected\n```\nIt is better to create separate route tables for public and private subnets so the design is clear.  ","x":350,"y":8250,"width":600,"height":305},
		{"id":"242ec39887b0d50c","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/3. Checklist for route table#**How to remember public and private subnet routes?**|How to remember public and private subnet routes?]]","x":-100,"y":8615,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"8d700641fb178c99","type":"text","text":"Use this simple rule:\n```text\nPublic subnet:\n0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway\nPrivate subnet:\nNo direct route to Internet Gateway\nPrivate subnet with outbound internet:\n0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway\n```\nSo:\n```text\nPublic subnet  = direct internet road\nPrivate subnet = no direct internet road\nNAT Gateway    = private subnet can go out, but internet cannot directly come in\n```\n**Simple idea**  \nA `Route Table` tells AWS where network traffic should go.\n```text\n10.0.0.0/16 → local\n```\nThis means traffic inside the `VPC` stays inside the `VPC`.\n```text\n0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway\n```\nThis means traffic goes directly to the internet.\n```text\n0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway\n```\nThis means private resources can go out to the internet without becoming directly public.  \nThe easiest way to remember it:\n```text\nRoute Table = traffic map\nDestination = where traffic wants to go\nTarget      = where AWS sends the traffic\nlocal       = inside the VPC\nIGW         = direct internet road\nNAT Gateway = private outbound internet road\n```","x":350,"y":8615,"width":600,"height":1075}
	],
